## Context

The README serves two overlapping audiences arriving from two surfaces.
GitHub visitors are evaluating whether to adopt the tool — they scan the
repo, read the README top-to-bottom, and decide. npm visitors land on the
package page after finding `@aidemd-mcp/server` in search results or a
link — they see the README rendered by npm's markdown engine, which
supports less than GitHub (no collapsible details for critical content, no
relative links, degraded callout styling). Both audiences need the same
core information: what AIDE is, how to install it in their specific
client, and what tools it provides. Neither audience wants a methodology
tutorial — that is what `aide_init` delivers after the install.

The MCP ecosystem has a specific install friction profile. Each client
stores its config in a different file at a different path, and VS Code
uses a different JSON root key (`"servers"`) than every other client
(`"mcpServers"`). Users who copy a config block for the wrong client get
a silent failure. Claude Desktop has an additional failure mode: it does
not inherit the terminal's PATH, so users with nvm or Homebrew Node
installations get "command not found" errors that are never explained in
most MCP server READMEs. These are the documented friction points the
README must address head-on.

The tool roster changes between releases, and every tool already has a
full reference page on aidemd.dev — parameters, returns, guarantees —
kept current with the code. A tools list maintained by hand inside the
README drifts from that roster within a few releases. The top of the
page also carries more weight than the rest: answer engines and skimming
visitors take their idea of what AIDE is from the opening, so an opening
that names one component as the whole defines the project wrongly
everywhere it is quoted.

## Strategy

**Open with a plain definition of the framework, not a pitch for one
package.** The H1 stays the package name; the first paragraph says what
AIDE is — an open-source agentic SDLC framework for intent-driven,
spec-driven development — and names its parts: skills, subagents, the MCP
toolkit, the brain interface, the observer. ChatGPT draws 44% of its
citations from the first 30% of a page, and for a repo that is the README
opening (github-repo-discoverability research). Calling AIDE an MCP server
there teaches every engine the wrong category.

**Badges signal legitimacy and version currency.** Place npm version and
MIT license badges immediately below the H1, before the description. Add
npm weekly downloads once the package has meaningful traction. Skip CI/build
badges — no MCP server README in the research corpus uses them, and the
audience does not expect them (mcp-readme-npm-badges research). Keep the
Glama score badge: the largest awesome-MCP list merges a listing only with
a Glama backlink (mcp-directories research).

**Name the framework's parts in the features list, each linking its
aidemd.dev page.** No "What is AIDE?" section: the opening already defines
it, and an explainer pushes the install block down (mcp-readme-conventions
research, section order finding). One bullet per part — methodology docs,
skills and subagents for each phase, spec scaffolding, the MCP toolkit, the
brain interface, the observer — then one line pointing to the Learn pages.
Features never restate the tools list, which has its own section (user
decision 2026-11-28).

**Show a separate config block per client, not one block with a
footnote.** Use Pattern A from the conventions research: one H3 per
client under the Installation H2, each with its own copy-pasteable JSON
block showing the correct root key, the correct config file path, and
the same global launch. The clients to cover are Claude Code (CLI
command and .mcp.json), Claude Desktop (Mac and Windows paths), Cursor,
VS Code / Copilot (noting the `"servers"` root key difference), and
Windsurf. This is the largest section of the README and the most visited
— every character must be copy-pasteable without modification
(mcp-readme-config-blocks research, multi-client presentation finding).

**Launch from a global install in every client section, framed the same
way.** Each section shows a one-time `npm i -g @aidemd-mcp/server`, then
the bare `aidemd-mcp` command, and nothing else. The installed version
stays put until the user upgrades it, and the client starts the server
without an npx fetch at session start, which can overrun the 30-second
MCP connect timeout on Windows. No section offers an npx launch, floating
or pinned (user decision 2026-10-27, overriding the config-blocks
research's `npx @latest` recommendation).

**Include the `-y` flag in the one npx command the README keeps.** The
one-shot installer still runs through npx, pinned to the released
version. All official MCP server READMEs use `npx -y` to auto-confirm the
package install prompt; without it npx waits for a confirmation that a
scripted or non-interactive shell never answers, a silent install failure
(mcp-readme-conventions research).

**Add a PATH troubleshooting callout in the Claude Desktop section.**
Claude Desktop does not inherit the terminal PATH — users with
nvm/Homebrew Node can find the globally installed `aidemd-mcp` "command
not found" and silently churn. A blockquote callout (which degrades to a
plain blockquote on npm — acceptable) naming the problem and the fix (use
`which aidemd-mcp` to find the absolute path)
addresses the single most common MCP install failure
(mcp-readme-config-blocks research, PATH problem finding; install-cta-mechanics
research, friction map finding).

**Note the Claude Desktop restart requirement.** Claude Desktop requires
a full quit-and-reopen for config changes to take effect. This is the #2
documented failure mode. A one-line note in the Claude Desktop section
prevents it (mcp-readme-config-blocks research, reload requirement finding).

**Generate the tools list from the reference pages; never write it by
hand.** Each entry is one line: the tool's name linking its aidemd.dev
reference page, and the one-sentence description that page gives, in the
reference index's order. Parameters live on those pages, maintained with
the code; the README is the index to them, not a second copy. Because the
list is built from the same pages, a new, renamed or removed tool cannot
leave the README behind (user decision 2026-11-28, superseding the
tool-documentation research's full-parameter recommendation).

**Add a post-install "try it" block.** After the Tools section, include a
short block that tells the developer what to do after installing: "Run
`aide_init` in your agent to bootstrap the AIDE methodology into your
project, then try `/aide` to start the first phase." This closes the
install-to-value gap that silently loses users in dev-tool landings
(install-cta-mechanics research, post-install hand-off finding).

**Keep the Development section minimal.** Three commands (install, build,
test) in a code block. No contributing guide, no changelog — those belong
in separate files if needed. The README is for users, not contributors
(mcp-readme-conventions research, building section finding).

**Use absolute URLs for any cross-repo links.** Relative links work on
GitHub but break on the npm package page. Any link to files within the
repo (LICENSE, methodology docs) must use the full GitHub URL
(mcp-readme-npm-badges research, npm rendering finding).

**Avoid details/summary tags for any content a visitor needs to see.**
They are broken on npm. Use flat sections instead. The `> [!NOTE]`
callout syntax is acceptable — it renders as a styled callout on GitHub
and degrades to a readable blockquote on npm (mcp-readme-npm-badges
research, npm rendering finding).

## Good examples

An opening that defines the framework and names its parts:

> # @aidemd-mcp/server
>
> [![npm version](https://img.shields.io/npm/v/@aidemd-mcp/server.svg)](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@aidemd-mcp/server)
> [![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-yellow.svg)](https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT)
>
> Open-source agentic SDLC framework for intent-driven, spec-driven
> development. AIDE (aidemd) gives AI coding agents a production-grade
> software methodology to follow, with skills, subagents and an MCP server
> whose tools work with a cascading intent tree of `.aide` spec files.
> `brain.aide` is a backend-agnostic plugin interface that connects your
> AIDE projects to a central task board and coding playbook, so every agent
> follows your team's conventions. It ships with an observer to manage
> running sessions and prevent collisions between them.

A per-client config block that eliminates translation friction:

> ### Claude Code
>
> Install once, then add the server:
>
> ```bash
> npm i -g @aidemd-mcp/server
> claude mcp add aide aidemd-mcp
> ```
>
> ### VS Code / Copilot
>
> After `npm i -g @aidemd-mcp/server`, add to `.vscode/mcp.json`:
>
> ```json
> {
>   "servers": {
>     "aide": {
>       "command": "aidemd-mcp"
>     }
>   }
> }
> ```

Tools entries generated from their reference pages, each linking out:

> ## Tools
>
> - [`aide_discover`](https://aidemd.dev/docs/tools/aide_discover/) — Maps
>   where the project's .aide specs live, or gives one module's ancestor
>   chain and subtree, so agents read intent before code.
> - [`aide_validate`](https://aidemd.dev/docs/tools/aide_validate/) — Health
>   check for the spec layout, reporting orphaned or missing specs, plans
>   with no spec, naming conflicts, broken links and frontmatter problems.

A post-install block that bridges install to first value:

> ## Getting Started
>
> After adding the server to your MCP client, ask your agent to run
> `aide_init` to bootstrap the AIDE methodology into your project. This
> installs the methodology docs, scaffolds pipeline commands, and wires
> everything up.
>
> Then try: "Scaffold an intent spec for my authentication module" — the
> agent will use `aide_discover` to map your project and `aide_scaffold`
> to create the spec in the right place with the right naming conventions.

## Bad examples

A README that opens with a methodology explainer before the install block:

> ## What is AIDE?
>
> AIDE (Autonomous Intent-Driven Engineering) is a methodology that puts
> intent at the center of everything. It uses a three-layer model...
> [300 words of methodology detail]
>
> ## Installation
> ...

The visitor wanted to install, not to study. The methodology detail
belongs in the docs that `aide_init` delivers, not in the README that
must convince the visitor to install. By the time they reach the config
block, they have either scrolled past or left. The opposite misframe is
as wrong: "MCP server that brings intent-driven development to any
AI-powered IDE" is short, but it names one part of the framework as the
whole, and every answer engine that quotes it repeats the error.

A single config block labeled "Add to your MCP client config" with the
`mcpServers` root key and no per-client guidance:

> ```json
> {
>   "mcpServers": {
>     "aide": {
>       "command": "npx",
>       "args": ["@aidemd-mcp/server"]
>     }
>   }
> }
> ```

A VS Code user pastes this into `.vscode/mcp.json`, where the root key
should be `"servers"`, not `"mcpServers"`. The server silently fails to
load. The user assumes the package is broken and moves on. Meanwhile, the
`-y` flag is missing, so Claude Desktop users get a hung npx prompt they
never see. Two silent failures from one config block.

A hand-written tools section, tidy and plausible:

> ### aide_scaffold
>
> Create a new `.aide` file. **Inputs:** `directory` (string), `type`
> (intent, research, plan or todo).

The server registers more tools than the section lists, and the `type`
values shown are a release out of date. Nothing fails when it drifts; an
evaluating developer who finds a tool in their client that the README never
mentioned stops trusting the rest of the page.

A README that uses `<details>` tags to collapse the per-client config
blocks:

> <details>
> <summary>Claude Desktop</summary>
> [config block]
> </details>

On the npm package page, the details/summary tags are broken — the
content is either always expanded or invisible depending on npm's
renderer version. The most-visited section of the README is now
inaccessible on one of the two primary surfaces the README serves.

## References

- `research/mcp-readme/mcp-readme-conventions` -- Canonical section order (title, badges, features, install, tools, dev, license) and the Pattern A multi-client presentation finding that drives the per-client config block strategy.
- `research/mcp-readme/mcp-readme-config-blocks` -- The `mcpServers` vs `"servers"` root key difference, PATH troubleshooting requirement for Claude Desktop, and the restart-after-config-change requirement. Its `npx @latest` launch recommendation is superseded by the user's 2026-10-27 decision (session decision #56, released 0.15.0): global install only, launched by the bare `aidemd-mcp` command, no npx launch.
- `research/mcp-readme/mcp-readme-tool-documentation` -- The one-sentence tool description convention. Its full-parameter recommendation for small servers is superseded by the user's 2026-11-28 decision (generated one-line list linking each tool's reference page).
- `research/seo-aeo/2026-11-25-github-repo-discoverability` -- README first paragraph as the plain category definition; ChatGPT draws 44% of citations from the first 30% of a page, which for a repo is the README opening.
- `research/seo-aeo/2026-11-28-mcp-directories-and-awesome-lists` -- The punkpeye awesome-MCP list requires a Glama backlink to merge a listing (keep the Glama badge); Smithery was acquired and its proxy shut down 2026-10-31, so no Smithery badge.
- User decisions, relayed by the orchestrator 2026-11-28 -- Generated tools list over hand-written; AIDE framed as a framework of methodology, skills, subagents, MCP toolkit, brain interface and observer; the verbatim opening paragraph; features linking aidemd.dev pages and the Learn pages; pipeline steps called phases.
- `research/mcp-readme/mcp-readme-npm-badges` -- Badge placement convention (below H1, before description), recommended badge stack (version + license minimum), npm rendering constraints (no details/summary, absolute URLs required, callout degradation acceptable).
- `research/aidemd-dev/install-cta-mechanics` -- The MCP install friction map (config location confusion, reload requirement, PATH inheritance failure, cross-client ambiguity), the post-install "try it" block pattern from Stripe/shadcn precedent, and the v1 scope note confirming Claude Code as primary target.
